<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis XIII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1601">1601</a>–<a href="/years/1643">1643</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis XIII, son of Henry IV, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1610. In 1620 he united Béarn and Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> with the French crown and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> there. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> as chief minister from 1624 he took the Huguenot stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1628 and entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1635, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1640. In 1642 the prince of Sedan ceded his principality to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis XIII of France" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_XIII,_rey_de_Francia_(Philippe_de_Champaigne).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles IV’s alliance with the Habsburgs and with <b>Louis XIII</b>’s rebellious brother Gaston of Orléans, who secretly married the duke’s sister Marguerite, provoked French invasion in 1633; the duchy was occupied for most of the next three decades and ravaged by war, plague and famine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cardinal Richelieu, chief minister of <b>Louis XIII</b> from 1624 to 1642, and Cardinal Mazarin after him extended the use of intendants, royal commissioners in the provinces, and Mazarin survived the revolt of the Fronde (1648–1653).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Lower Navarre, north of the mountains, remained with the Albrets, whose heir Henry III became King Henry IV of France; <b>Louis XIII</b> formally united it to the French crown in 1620.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Led by Pau Claris, the Generalitat proclaimed a short-lived Catalan Republic in January 1641 and then accepted <b>Louis XIII of France</b> as count of Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 23 January 1641 the Catalan authorities recognised <b>Louis XIII of France</b> as Count of Barcelona, ending the republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/catalan-republic-1641">Catalan Republic (1641)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Louis XIII</b> took Arras in 1640, and the Treaty of the Pyrenees of 1659 gave most of the county to the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-artois">County of Artois</a></li></ul></section>
